{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 37, "model_name": "nvidia/llama-3.3-nemotron-super-49b-v1", "content": "i will provide a detailed review of the movie \"the social network\" in all lower case as requested.\n\n**movie title:** the social network\n**director:** david fincher\n**release year:** 2010\n**genre:** biographical drama\n\n**review:**\n\ni recently watched \"the social network\" and found it to be a compelling, albeit somewhat biased, portrayal of the founding of facebook. the film's narrative is presented in a non-linear fashion, jumping back and forth between mark zuckerberg's (played by jesse eisenberg) time at harvard and the subsequent legal battles over the ownership of facebook.\n\n**acting:**\n- jesse eisenberg delivers a strong performance as mark zuckerberg, capturing the character's introverted and somewhat awkward personality, as well as his intellectual arrogance.\n- andrew garfield, as eduard savelievich (co-founder of facebook), brings a sense of vulnerability and betrayal to the screen, making his character's plight sympathetic.\n- the twins, cameron and tyler winklevoss (played by armie hammer), are portrayed as entitled harvard elites, which somewhat simplifies their real-life complexity.\n\n**direction and cinematography:**\ndavid fincher's direction is meticulous, with a keen eye for detail that immerses the viewer in the early 2000s harvard environment. the cinematography is stark and minimalist, reflecting the tech-driven, emotionally distant world of the characters.\n\n**script:**\naaron sorkin's script is fast-paced and dialogue-driven, which suits the subject matter well. however, it's notable that the script takes creative liberties with the true events, often portraying mark zuckerberg in a negative light without providing a fully balanced view.\n\n**music:**\ntrent reznor and atticus ross's soundtrack is haunting and perfectly complements the on-screen tension, especially during scenes depicting the intense 